'''CVE-2026-109618''' and '''CVE-2026-109619''' are two [[Vulnerability (computer security)|vulnerabilities]] in [[PHP]] that allow [[Arbitrary code execution|remote code execution]] and are together known as '''PHP2Shell'''. Their existence was publicly announced on 20 October 2026.<ref name="oct20">{{Cite web |date=2026-10-20 |title=PHP: News Archive - 20 Oct 2026 |url=https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-20-1 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.php.net |language=en}}</ref> An emergency patch and details about the vulnerabilites were released on the following day.<ref name="oct21">{{Cite web |date=2026-10-21 |title=PHP: News Archive - 21 Oct 2026 |url=https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-21-1|access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.php.net |language=en}}</ref>

==Discovery==
The vulnerabilities were independently discovered by the California-based security firm Calif as part of the [[OpenAI]] "Patch the Planet" project, and by the Israeli-American cloud security company [[Wiz (company)|Wiz, Inc]].<ref name="openai">{{cite press release|title=Patch the Planet: global response coordination as agents uncover critical vulnerabilities |url=https://openai.com/index/coordinated-disclosure-of-critical-vulnerabilities/ |website=OpenAI |access-date=21 October 2026 |date=21 October 2026}}</ref> According to Wiz, versions as early as PHP 5.4, all the way through to PHP 8, are affected.<ref name="wiz">{{Cite web |title=PHP2Shell: AI uncovers critical RCE vulnerabilities affecting 70% of the Internet |url=https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce |access-date=2026-10-21}}</ref> 

[[File:Wikimedia Phabricator PHP2Shell.png|thumb|Wikimedia's [[Phabricator]] instance as seen hours before the patch, having been disabled as a precaution]]

The vulnerability was rated 9.2 (critical) out of 10 under [[Common Vulnerability Scoring System|CVSS]] v4.0 by PHP developers,<ref>{{Cite web |title=php.announce: [ANNOUNCE] Upcoming Critical PHP Security Release |url=https://news-web.php.net/php.announce/509 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=news-web.php.net}}</ref> while security firm watchTowr rated it a full 10.0.<ref name="Website-3">{{Cite web |last=Abrams |first=Lawrence |title=PHP warns admins of two critical flaws, emergency patches coming tomorrow |url=https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/php-warns-admins-of-two-critical-flaws-emergency-patches-coming-tomorrow/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=BleepingComputer |language=en-us}}</ref>

==Patches==
The maintainers of PHP released patched versions of PHP 8.2, 8.3, 8.4, and 8.5.<ref name="oct21" /> Source code patches were released for version 7.4, 8.0, and 8.1, but no official updated binaries for those versions were released as they are considered [[Software release life cycle#End-of-life|end of life]].<ref name="oct21" /> Both vulnerabilities were published through GitHub security advisories<ref>{{cite web|url=https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w|title=RCE via OOM corruption|date=2026-10-21|access-date=2026-10-21}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp|title=RCE via stale global state|date=2026-10-21|access-date=2026-10-21}}</ref> on 21 October at 13:34 UTC, 26 minutes before the announced release time, with the PHP website updated shortly thereafter.

The PHP maintainers announced the update early and before release, after news of the vulnerabilities leaked on the [[Hacker News]] forums and were confirmed by the [[X (social network)|X]] account of cybersecurity company watchTowr labs.<ref name="Khandelwal2026-1">{{cite news |last1=Khandelwal |first1=Swati |title=PHP2Shell: Two Unpatched Critical PHP RCE Flaws (CVSS 9.2) Trigger Emergency Security Alert |url=https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/php2shell-two-unpatched-critical-php.html |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=The Hacker News |date=21 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref> The early notice on PHP.net encouraged users to prepare and update as soon as the patch was released.<ref name="Khandelwal2026-1" /><ref name="oct20" /> One security firm, Sansec, expected "mass attacks to start within 15 minutes of the release".<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Goodin |first=Dan |date=2026-10-21 |title=PHP warns it plans to release 2 emergency patches imminently |url=https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/php-maintainers-tell-web-devs-to-prepare-themselves-for-critical-security-patches/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=Ars Technica |language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261021121524/https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/php-maintainers-tell-web-devs-to-prepare-themselves-for-critical-security-patches/|archive-date=2026-10-21|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Proof of concept|PoCs]], write-ups, and partial mitigations started circulating on the Internet before the end of the embargo.<ref name="Website-3" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Only for the curious... |url=https://jackalsyn.com/posts/php-up-2026.html |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=jackalsyn.com}}</ref> 

[[Cloudflare]] has reported signs of early exploitation prior to public patch release, and deployed temporary [[web application firewall]] (WAF) rules as an attempted [[stopgap]] mitigation against the vulnerability.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Knecht |first1=Dane |title=Dane Knecht 🦭 (@dok2001) on X |url=https://x.com/dok2001/status/2112811965735202970 |website=X (formerly Twitter) |date=21 October 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-10-21 |title=Hold on to your butts: Web admins braced for emergency PHP updates to plug leaked exploits |url=https://www.pcgamer.com/software/security/we-expect-mass-attacks-to-start-within-15-minutes-of-the-release-web-admins-braced-for-emergency-php-updates-to-plug-leaked-high-severity-holes/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |work=PC Gamer |language=en}}</ref>

== Impact ==
Following an advice from [[Computer emergency response team|CERT]]-EU, many organizations across Europe took their websites offline as a precaution while the patches were pending.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Satter |first=Raphael |last2=Pardesi |first2=Rajveer |last3=Marchandon |first3=Leo |last4=Satter |first4=Raphael |last5=Marchandon |first5=Leo |date=2026-10-21 |title=Government websites go dark as software flaw sparks security scramble |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/eu-agency-warns-websites-may-need-be-taken-offline-php-prepares-patches-2026-10-21/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-21 |title=Websites taken down as critical PHP cyber-security update issued |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmly0lg3w5x2o |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |title=Major websites go offline as critical PHP vulnerability triggers security fears |url=https://www.brusselstimes.com/belgium/2371464/major-websites-go-offline-as-critical-php-vulnerability-triggers-security-fears |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=The Brussels Times |language=en}}</ref>

